WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
The National Museum of Korea is pleased to present the newly-renovated Donated Collection Ⅰ.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
This is the first outcome of the Donated Collection Gallery Renovation Project, which will continue into 2023.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
It is a space where you can gain an understanding of the meaning and value of donations.

4
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
The "The Study for Sharing," the first space you will encounter in the exhibition room,

5
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
is composed of a lounge where you can appreciate donated items that bear diverse stories

6
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
and an archive for sharing donation-related information.

7
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Major exhibits include "Blue and White Porcelain Jar with Dragon and Cloud," donated by Park Byoung-rae;

8
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
"Boy Attendant," donated by Kim Chong-hak;

9
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
"Iron-glazed White Porcelain Seated Figure" donated by Lee Hong-kun;

10
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.000
and "Rock," donated by Kim Whanki.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
All in one place, you can see more than 200 donated items with different historical and cultural backgrounds.

12
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
In the archive, visitors can watch donor documentary videos

13
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
containing numerous stories along with information on the donors and their donations.

14
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:48.000
In the media space, the meaning of donation can be explored more deeply

15
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.000
from the perspective of donors, cultural heritage specialists, museum staff, and visitors with a focus on "memories."

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.000
"The Path of Sharing," which unfolds like a galaxy, is a space for commemorating donors.

17
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Lastly, the space for "Greek Helmet Donated by Son Kee Chung"

18
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
shed lights on a Greek helmet donated by Son Kee Chung in 1994.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Along with the touching story of the 1936 Berlin Olympics, held during the Japanese colonial period,

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
it describes how the helmet ended up being donated to the National Museum of Korea.

21
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.000
To take one step closer to becoming a "Museum for All,"

22
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:44.000
we also installed features for the culturally vulnerable such as braille information panels, a subtitle text-to-speech system for the visually impaired,

23
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
easy-to-understand information guides, and a bronze helmet to provide a more tactile experience.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.000
We hope that many people will visit the newly-refurbished Donated Collection Gallery

25
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
to feel the kindheartedness of donors and enjoy the practice of sharing.
